## Describe the bug

When the joint is beyond a soft limit, `JointSoftLimiter` lets a position command jump straight to the soft limit within a single update, which breaks the hard velocity limit.

In `JointSoftLimiter::on_enforce()` ([joint_soft_limiter.cpp](https://github.com/ros-controls/ros2_control/blob/master/joint_limits/src/joint_soft_limiter.cpp)), the position command is clamped to the soft interval, which is narrowed by the spring velocity bounds. That result is then intersected with the saturation limiter's interval `previous_command ± max_velocity * dt`. If the two intervals don't overlap, the saturation interval is **dropped** and only the soft interval is used. This fallback was added in #3275 to avoid the `std::clamp(lo > hi)` assertion with GCC 15.

Beyond a soft limit the two intervals never overlap, so the command is set to the soft limit regardless of `max_velocity`. Example: hard limits ±1.57 rad, soft limits ±1.40 rad, `k_position` 10, `max_velocity` 1.0 rad/s, dt 0.01 s, actual position 1.45 rad:

- soft interval: `[1.40, 1.40]`
- saturation interval: `[1.44, 1.46]`
- result: **1.40 rad**, a jump of 0.05 rad in 10 ms (5 rad/s) even though the command was to *hold* 1.45 rad

Velocity commands behave as intended in the same situation: they bring the joint back at ≈1°/s.

This can happen when the joint is pushed beyond a soft limit (e.g., gravity, external forces, an effort-controlled phase) or when switching to a position controller there.

## To Reproduce

Failing test for `joint_limits/test/test_joint_soft_limiter.cpp`:

```cpp
// Beyond a soft limit, the soft interval and the velocity-reachable interval of the saturation
// limiter do not overlap. The position command must still respect the hard velocity limit.
TEST_F(JointSoftLimiterTest, position_beyond_soft_limit_respects_velocity_limit)
{
  SetupNode("soft_joint_limiter");
  ASSERT_TRUE(Load());

  joint_limits::JointLimits limits;
  limits.has_position_limits = true;
  limits.min_position = -1.57;
  limits.max_position = 1.57;
  limits.has_velocity_limits = true;
  limits.max_velocity = 1.0;
  joint_limits::SoftJointLimits soft_limits;
  soft_limits.min_position = -1.40;
  soft_limits.max_position = 1.40;
  soft_limits.k_position = 10.0;
  ASSERT_TRUE(Init(limits, soft_limits));

  const rclcpp::Duration period = rclcpp::Duration::from_seconds(0.01);
  desired_state_ = {};
  actual_state_ = {};
  // the previous command is seeded with the actual position, the joint is commanded to hold it
  actual_state_.position = 1.45;
  desired_state_.position = 1.45;
  joint_limiter_->enforce(actual_state_, desired_state_, period);
  EXPECT_LE(
    std::abs(desired_state_.position.value() - 1.45),
    limits.max_velocity * period.seconds() + COMMON_THRESHOLD);
}
```

Output on current master:

```
Expected: (std::abs(desired_state_.position.value() - 1.45)) <= (limits.max_velocity * period.seconds() + COMMON_THRESHOLD), actual: 0.050000000000000044 vs 0.010001
```

## Expected behavior

The hard velocity limit should take precedence. If the intervals don't overlap, use the bound of the saturation interval that is closest to the soft interval: the joint then moves back toward the soft limit at no more than `max_velocity` (or at the spring/1°/s rate, to match velocity commands).

Note that the existing test `position_soft_limits_no_overlap_with_velocity_reachable_range` (from #3275) currently asserts the jump (2.0 → 0.75 within one period with `max_velocity` 1.0) and would need to be adapted. Any fix must still never call `std::clamp` with `lo > hi`.

## Environment

- ros2_control master (rolling)
- Found while documenting the limiters in #3166 (review comment https://github.com/ros-controls/ros2_control/pull/3166#discussion_r4620982317)

